The  utility  queries  and  displays  in‐core status of specified
files.  Note that the result can already be stale when being out‐
put due to other activities in the system.   Thus  it  should  be
used only for advisory purposes.  The utility accepts the follow‐
ing  options.   The  quiet mode.  Outputs nothing unless the file
has in‐core pages.  The  summary  mode.   Only  shows  number  of
pages.  The following example shows that and are fully cached in‐
core  while the other executables are not in‐core.  numbers shown
in the default output are page indexes in the file  of  each  in‐
core  pages.   %  fincore  /bin/c*  /bin/cat:  0 1 2 3 /bin/chio:
/bin/chmod: /bin/cp: 0 1 2 3 4 5 /bin/cpio: /bin/csh:  %  fincore
‐s /bin/c* /bin/cat: 4 / 4 in‐core pages (100.00%) /bin/chio: 0 /
5  in‐core  pages (0.00%) /bin/chmod: 0 / 3 in‐core pages (0.00%)
/bin/cp: 6 / 6 in‐core pages (100.00%) /bin/cpio: 0 / 36  in‐core
pages  (0.00%) /bin/csh: 0 / 41 in‐core pages (0.00%) The utility
is written by The concept of page cache is an implementation  de‐
tail  of the kernel.  The utility works using some assumptions on
the current implementation.  Thus it might stop working in a  fu‐
ture version of The amount of CPU time the current implementation
of  utility would take is roughly proportional to the file sizes.
Ideally it should be proportional to the number of in‐core pages.
